From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:49:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Frictions filter: price the spread and commission against max
 profit

Every other gauge on the ticket asks whether a trade is likely to WIN. This one
asks whether winning is worth anything once you have paid to get in and out --
the only measure that separates a thin structure from a good one before the
market has an opinion.

The four 0DTE candidates on 2-3 Sep 2026 are why. All four finished green and
looked interchangeable on outcome; on this number they are not close:

  QQQ 707/710/715 call fly      5.1%   acceptable
  SPY 769/772/778 call fly      7.0%   acceptable
  SPY 769/771 bull call        22.4%   prohibitive
  QQQ 700/703 711/714 condor   28.8%   prohibitive

The condor held the HIGHEST probability of the four (82%) and the widest cushion
(1.35x EM), and still gives up more than a quarter of its best case to execution.
A payoff that thin is not a probability problem and no POP repairs it.

  data.js       comboQuote() rebuilds a combo bid/ask from per-leg quotes -- buy
                pays the ask on longs, receives the bid on shorts. Reproduces the
                TWS Strategy Builder quote to within a cent on flies, verticals
                and condors. computeFrictions() charges the spread round trip
                (you cross it twice) plus commission on every leg both ways, over
                max profit per contract. Bands: <5% clean, <10% acceptable,
                <20% heavy, else prohibitive. Shared, so 45DTE can adopt it.
  calc0dte.js   computes it and raises ONE warning at the prohibitive band.
                Leg count comes from the payoff's legs, not the suggestion's -- a
                4-leg dual-EM suggestion is two alternative 2-leg spreads, and
                billing four legs of commission on it is wrong.
  bridge        reqMktData already carried the quote alongside the greeks and we
                were discarding it; a tickPrice listener keeps it, and the
                endpoint returns a combo bid/ask when EVERY leg quoted (a partial
                sum is a wrong number, not an approximate one).
  EnginePanel   combo bid/ask fill from Fetch Greeks, or type them from TWS with
                no bridge at all. Absent a quote the gauge says so rather than
                inventing a spread.

Display only: it drives one warning and no gate. Nothing about score, EV, Kelly,
P(max loss) or any blocker changes -- asserted directly in the tests.

Verified: vite build clean; node --check clean on the bridge; 14/14 vitest specs
pass. Two of them earned their place -- comboQuote accepted a leg with a null bid
because Number(null) is 0 and passes isFinite, silently summing a missing quote
as zero; and rounding both per-contract and at contract scale produced "$10 each,
$99 for ten", so the helper now returns exact dollars and the UI rounds once.

